% \subsection{A fixed board on a landscape page}
%
% A landscape page increases |\linewidth| and is therefore useful when a
% workflow has five or more stages that would be too narrow in portrait
% orientation. The \textsf{pdflscape} package establishes the rotated page and
% records the appropriate PDF rotation metadata; the board itself requires no
% rotation commands. Page margins should be chosen before the board is opened,
% and |columnsep| can then be reduced modestly if the available width is still
% insufficient.
%
% The board must use the fixed layout. The |break| option is deliberately
% absent because its \textsf{paracol}-based pagination is intended for ordinary
% portrait text pages and is not compatible with this use. A fixed board is
% kept as one indivisible object, including its header and body rows. It will
% therefore move to the next page when the remaining vertical space is too
% small. If the complete board is taller than one landscape page, the content
% should be reduced or presented as a separate page-breakable portrait board.
%
% Concise stage names are especially important when six columns share the page.
% The example uses |Build| and |Docs| as familiar abbreviations for
% implementation and documentation. Less familiar abbreviations should be
% defined in the surrounding text so that the board remains understandable
% without relying on source-code comments.
%
% \begin{samepage}
% \begin{lstlisting}
% \documentclass[a4paper]{article}
% \usepackage[margin=18mm]{geometry}
% \usepackage{pdflscape}
% \usepackage[columnsep=2mm]{kanban}
%
% \begin{document}
% \begin{landscape}
% \section*{Release workflow}
%
% % Deliberately omit the break option in a landscape environment.
% \begin{kanban}[
%   columns={Ideas,Selected,Build,Review,Docs,Released},
%   mode=light
% ]
%   \kanbantask[deadline=01/01/2000]
%     {Ideas}{Collect feature requests}
%   \kanbantask{Ideas}{Record user feedback}
%   \kanbantask[prio=high,
%     due=07/18/2026]
%     {Selected}{Approve the release scope}
%   \kanbantask[author=Alice,deadline=12/31/2099]
%     {Build}{Complete the changes}
%   \kanbantask[author=Bob]{Review}{Run the acceptance review}
%   \kanbantask[author=Carol]{Docs}{Update the user manual}
%   \kanbantask[completed]{Released}{Publish the archive}
% \end{kanban}
% \end{landscape}
% \end{document}
% \end{lstlisting}
% \end{samepage}
% The supporting package is needed only by a document that requests rotated
% pages; it is not a dependency of \textsf{kanban}. The excerpt shows the
% structural pattern. The standalone example adds three tasks to every stage so
% that the use of the available landscape area can be assessed realistically.
%
% \paragraph{Rendered output.}
% The next page is generated directly in a |landscape| environment. The board
% uses six fixed columns, short one-line headings, three cards per column, and
% no |break| option. Equal body heights make the workflow read as one coherent
% table even though individual task cards have different amounts of metadata.
%
% \begin{landscape}
% \section*{Landscape release workflow}
