% \section{Implementation}
%
% \subsection{Public command contracts}
%
% The implementation distinguishes persistent defaults, one-board state, and
% task data. The |kanban| environment establishes a collection scope, copies
% the persistent defaults, applies its local options, and selects one of the
% two renderers. It is the only public operation that creates board state.
% |\kanbantask|, or its exact short alias |\kbtask| when available, may mutate
% that state only while a board is active; otherwise it reports an error and
% discards the declaration. A nested board is rejected at
% its entry point, and its task declarations enter a temporary discard state so
% that the primary structural error is not obscured by secondary diagnostics.
%
% |\kanbansetup| changes persistent defaults for boards that begin later. When
% the short name is unused, |\kbset| is defined by control-sequence equivalence,
% not by a forwarding wrapper. The same rule applies to
% |\kanbandefinepalette| and |\kbdefinepalette|, and to |\kanbantask| and
% |\kbtask|. Existing short definitions are preserved and diagnosed. Palette
% definition changes the registry; selection
% changes the current palette name, its background list, and any paired
% heading-foreground list. Redefining the selected name refreshes the
% persistent lists for later boards, whereas a board already in progress keeps
% its local snapshot. No generic |\task| command is defined,
% because that generic name has a material risk of collision with classes and
% unrelated packages.
%
% \subsection{Configuration and data collection}
%
% A board begins by copying the current document defaults for columns, palette,
% visual mode, date format, project start date, reference date, semantic labels,
% and column separation into board-local state.
% Its optional key list is then applied to that state. In particular, the
% |columns| key replaces the copied column list for one board without changing
% later boards. Every column name is trimmed and converted to uppercase. A
% board-local LaTeX3 property list maps each normalized name to a consecutive
% numeric identifier. Task lists and counters use the numeric identifier and a
% monotonically increasing board identifier, so user-provided names never form
% part of an internal control-sequence name. Duplicate normalized names are
% diagnosed before rendering. Column lists, palette backgrounds, and optional
% paired foregrounds are stored as LaTeX3 comma lists. Palette entries are
% selected cyclically by column position, so a palette may contain either
% fewer or more colors than a particular board requires. Configuration is
% parsed by the namespaced LaTeX3 key families |kanban/task|, |kanban/board|,
% and |kanban/setup|, with subordinate label families. Keeping these roles
% explicit avoids leaking a generic key family into the document namespace.
% Start and reference dates are deferred until the complete surrounding key
% list has resolved its date format, then validated, expanded, and stored in
% canonical ISO form. Package options are accumulated and passed through the
% same setup transaction rather than maintaining a second parser.
%
% Task declarations do not typeset cards immediately. Instead, each declaration
% normalizes and validates the target column, increments its counter, and
% appends a protected call to the card renderer to that column's list. Empty and
% unknown targets produce explicit errors and are not appended. Task text and
% metadata remain unexpanded while they are stored. Collection is global only
% for the lifetime of the active board, which means a declaration inside an
% ordinary TeX group is retained. The board identifier isolates that temporary
% state, and the generated names, colors, heading foregrounds, lists, and
% counters are explicitly undefined after rendering. The lists are expanded
% only when the environment closes. This
% deferred model preserves declaration order within each column and allows
% tasks for different columns to be interleaved freely in the document source.
% After the columns are resolved, the package also evaluates the complete gap
% width. Dimension syntax is checked before assignment, preventing malformed
% input from reaching TeX's number scanner. A value that would leave no positive
% width for cards is diagnosed once
% and replaced locally by |0pt| before either renderer is entered.
%
% \subsection{Rendering strategies}
%
% The fixed renderer creates two raster rows: one containing every heading and
% one containing every transparent column body. Row-wise equalization gives all
% headings the height of the tallest heading and all bodies the height of the
% fullest body. The complete raster is enclosed in a minipage. This makes the
% header and body rows indivisible, preventing an orphaned header row at the
% foot of a page. A fixed board that does not fit in the remaining space moves
% to the following page; a board taller than the page must instead use the
% page-breakable layout.
%
% When |break| is selected, the package constructs parallel \textsf{paracol}
% columns. Header heights are synchronized through a board-specific equal-height
% group, while the card lists remain outside an enclosing fixed-height box. Each
% column may therefore continue naturally on a later page. Headers are emitted
% once before the streams diverge and are not repeated on continuation pages;
% this uses only the supported \textsf{paracol} interface. The two renderers
% share the same card command and visual styles, but deliberately use different
% body-height and pagination rules.
%
% \subsection{Metadata and validation}
%
% Calendar and project-month classification is performed when a card is
% rendered. The deadline value is expanded into a temporary token list and is
% matched against exact numeric forms. All five supported calendar notations
% are reduced to the same year, month, and day components. Ambiguous year-last
% slash values are interpreted according to the active date-format snapshot,
% with a validity-based swap when only the opposite ordering forms a real
% Gregorian date. The components are checked against month lengths and leap-year
% rules before they are compared with the configured reference date, or the TeX
% compilation date when no fixed reference has been supplied, and normalized
% for display. Project-month values are classified only after a valid
% start date has been established. Project-month numbers are bounded before
% integer conversion. Canonical start-date storage separates project semantics
% from later display-format changes. Project-month boundaries use the start-day number where
% possible and the last valid day in shorter months, including the Gregorian
% leap-year rule. Every other value remains ordinary label text, which prevents
% descriptive or oversized labels from reaching numeric operations.
%
% Card surfaces, metadata chips, and deadline indicators are named
% \textsf{tcolorbox} styles. Seven empty, namespaced public styles are applied
% after the structural defaults, which permits controlled presentation changes
% without exposing internal environment names. The column-body style is used
% by the fixed renderer only; the other six styles are shared by both
% renderers. Semantic label text is copied
% into the same board snapshot as the other persistent configuration. Built-in
% header and semantic color pairs have been selected against a 4.5:1 reference
% contrast threshold. The clock symbol is a small inline TikZ drawing.
% Internal LaTeX2e control sequences consistently use the |kanban@| or |kb@|
% prefix, while LaTeX3 variables and functions use the |__kanban| module name.
% The public namespace is limited to the environment and commands documented in
% the user-interface sections. Nested boards are detected before local board
% state is initialized, preventing an inner environment from corrupting the
% active outer collection. The regression suite verifies configuration scope,
% diagnostics, canonical date snapshots, fixed reference dates, metadata flag
% semantics, malformed dimensions, compatibility with the declared LaTeX format,
% all three supported engines, all five date formats, explicit custom heading
% foregrounds, full-width cards, synchronized break-layout headings, actual
% cross-page continuation, and impossible-gap recovery. When Poppler is
% available, continuous integration also rasterizes the manual and the first
% page of every example and retains those images for visual review; exact pixel
% equality is intentionally not asserted across differing TeX and rasterizer
% releases. The release archive is inspected for its required source tests.
%
% \subsection{Bootstrap state}
%
% Bootstrap code establishes the conditions under which every later subsystem
% operates. It identifies the package, loads the packages on which rendering
% depends, and allocates the persistent and board-local state used by the
% configuration, collection, validation, and rendering stages. This subsection
% presents each new kind of definition separately. In every case, the
% functional responsibility is stated first, followed by the implementation
% method and the meaning of the LaTeX commands used to express it.
%
% \paragraph{Package declaration and runtime dependencies.}
% The first responsibility is to make the generated style file identifiable
% and to prevent it from running on a LaTeX format older than the declared
% compatibility boundary. The package must also load each facility that its
% public behavior requires rather than relying on another package to load that
% facility incidentally.
%
% The docstrip guard |%<*package>| begins the part of the documented source
% that is copied into |kanban.sty|; each source part opens and closes its own
% guarded region, and |kanban.ins| assembles the parts in dependency order
% (this file, then the registry, date, configuration, renderer, and board
% parts). Within a guarded region,
% |\NeedsTeXFormat| declares the minimum LaTeX format date.
% |\ProvidesPackage| records the package name, release date, version, and short
% description. LaTeX prints this information in the compilation log and uses
% it when package versions are requested. Each |\RequirePackage| loads one
% declared dependency, while allowing LaTeX to avoid loading a dependency a
% second time if it is already present. Finally, |\tcbuselibrary| activates
% optional components of |tcolorbox| that are not part of its base interface.
%    \begin{macrocode}
%<*package>
\NeedsTeXFormat{LaTeX2e}[2020-10-01]
\ProvidesPackage{kanban}[2026/07/18 v1.0.0 Kanban project boards]
\RequirePackage{xcolor}
\RequirePackage{etoolbox}
\RequirePackage{tikz}
\RequirePackage{tcolorbox}
\RequirePackage{paracol}
\tcbuselibrary{skins,raster}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The dependencies have distinct responsibilities. |xcolor| provides named
% colors and color expressions. |etoolbox| supplies booleans, string tests,
% expandable list construction, and other LaTeX2e programming utilities.
% |tikz| draws the small clock symbol used by deadlines. |tcolorbox| constructs
% headers, cards, chips, and the fixed-board raster. |paracol| provides the
% independent, page-breakable column streams used by the |break| renderer.
% The |skins| library enables the required box decoration features, and the
% |raster| library provides the row-and-column layout for fixed boards.
%
% \paragraph{Built-in color constants.}
% The next definitions establish the package's semantic colors and the colors
% from which the built-in palettes are assembled. Defining them once gives the
% implementation stable symbolic names: later code refers to
% |kanbanHigh| or |kanbanSlate| instead of repeating numerical color values.
% This also keeps semantic meaning separate from the commands that draw a chip
% or heading.
%
% |\definecolor| is provided by |xcolor|. Its first argument creates the color
% name, the second selects the input model, and the third supplies the value in
% that model. The |HTML| model accepts six hexadecimal digits in red--green--
% blue order. These are ordinary named colors after their definitions, so they
% may be used by |tcolorbox|, TikZ, and standard LaTeX color commands.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\definecolor{kanbanInk}{HTML}{263238}
\definecolor{kanbanHigh}{HTML}{B23A48}
\definecolor{kanbanMed}{HTML}{E8A06A}
\definecolor{kanbanLow}{HTML}{477455}
\definecolor{kanbanSlate}{HTML}{4C5A6B}
\definecolor{kanbanBlue}{HTML}{3B7CA6}
\definecolor{kanbanTeal}{HTML}{267363}
\definecolor{kanbanSage}{HTML}{4F7D43}
\definecolor{kanbanAmber}{HTML}{876515}
\definecolor{kanbanClay}{HTML}{9B5030}
\definecolor{kanbanRose}{HTML}{AF5C74}
\definecolor{kanbanPlum}{HTML}{7C5C95}
\definecolor{kanbanMutedBlue}{HTML}{52758C}
\definecolor{kanbanMutedGreen}{HTML}{597C6E}
\definecolor{kanbanMutedPurple}{HTML}{746680}
\definecolor{kanbanMutedBrown}{HTML}{8A6F5A}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The three semantic colors represent overdue or high-priority work, work due
% at the active reference date, and completed or low-priority work. The other
% names are palette entries. Text colors are not hard-coded into the palette
% registry here because a later starred palette definition may provide an
% explicit foreground for each background.
%
% \paragraph{Boolean flags, board identity, and dimensions.}
% Rendering needs a small set of state flags. They record whether a board uses
% the page-breakable renderer, whether task declarations currently belong to
% an active board, whether the environment should render when it closes, and
% whether tasks inside a rejected nested board must be discarded. Two further
% flags carry the result of deadline classification. A unique board number
% prevents temporary data belonging to different boards from sharing names,
% and dedicated dimensions hold the persistent gap, its board-local snapshot,
% a validation candidate, and the combined width of all gaps.
%
% |\newbool| is an |etoolbox| definition command. It creates a named Boolean
% that is subsequently changed with |\booltrue| or |\boolfalse| and inspected
% with |\ifbool| or |\ifboolexpr|. |\newcounter| allocates a LaTeX counter;
% unlike a raw count register, it can be advanced through the standard counter
% interface. The internal macro |\kanban@boardgroup| stores the printable value
% associated with the active counter. |\newlength| allocates a skip register,
% and |\setlength| assigns the initial document-wide column gap. Separate
% registers are important here: validation can inspect a candidate without
% modifying a previously accepted value.
% \Needspace{16\baselineskip}
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newbool{kanban@break}
\newbool{kanban@active}
\newbool{kanban@render}
\newbool{kanban@discardtasks}
\newbool{kb@overdue}
\newbool{kb@duenow}
\newcounter{kanban@board}
\def\kanban@boardgroup{0}
\newlength\kanban@columnsep
\newlength\kanban@boardcolumnsep
\newlength\kanban@candidatecolumnsep
\newlength\kanban@totalgaps
\setlength\kanban@columnsep{2mm}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The |@| in these names marks them as internal LaTeX2e control sequences. It
% is a letter while a package file is read, but it is not normally a letter in
% a user's document. This convention reduces accidental use without presenting
% the names as a supported public API.
%
% \paragraph{Persistent defaults and board-local scalar snapshots.}
% Configuration values such as the start date, reference date, date format,
% visual mode, card colors, and semantic labels are simple token values. Most
% occur in pairs: one member is the persistent default for later boards, and
% the other is the snapshot used by the board currently being rendered. This
% separation implements the documented package--setup--board precedence and
% prevents a local override from altering subsequent boards.
%
% These internal scalar values are initialized with the TeX primitive |\def|.
% A definition such as |\def\kanban@dateformat{us}| creates a parameterless
% macro whose replacement text is |us|. Such macros are appropriate for small
% values that will be copied or reassigned frequently. Unlike |\newcommand|,
% |\def| does not check whether a command already exists; that is acceptable
% for private, package-owned names established in one controlled bootstrap
% location. Public commands are defined later with checking interfaces such as
% |\NewDocumentCommand|. Because the following definitions are executed at
% package load time, they establish document-wide initial values. Assignments
% made inside a board environment can remain local to that environment's TeX
% group.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\kanban@startdate{}
\def\kanban@thestart{}
\def\kanban@referencedate{}
\def\kanban@thereference{}
\def\kanban@dateformat{us}
\def\kanban@boarddateformat{us}
\def\kanban@mode{light}
\def\kanban@boardmode{light}
\def\kanban@textcolor{kanbanInk}
\def\kanban@cardcolor{white}
\def\kanban@bordercolor{black!15}
\def\kanban@authorback{black!6}
\def\kanban@authortext{black!58}
\def\kanban@deadlineback{black!7}
\def\kanban@deadlinetext{black!62}
\def\kanban@headingtext{white}
\def\kanban@hightext{white}
\def\kanban@mediumtext{kanbanInk}
\def\kanban@lowtext{white}
\def\kanban@highlabel{HIGH}
\def\kanban@mediumlabel{MED}
\def\kanban@lowlabel{LOW}
\def\kanban@donelabel{DONE}
\def\kanban@boardhighlabel{HIGH}
\def\kanban@boardmediumlabel{MED}
\def\kanban@boardlowlabel{LOW}
\def\kanban@boarddonelabel{DONE}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% Empty date macros mean that no fixed date has been supplied. The default
% reference then comes from TeX's compilation date. The color macros contain
% either a named color or an |xcolor| expression and are expanded only when a
% box is rendered. The four persistent labels and their four board-local
% counterparts allow semantic wording to be customized without changing the
% priority and completion logic.
%
% \paragraph{LaTeX3 syntax and typed state containers.}
% Palettes and columns require structured data rather than isolated scalar
% macros. Palette names must map to lists of colors, columns must map to numeric
% identifiers, and parsing routines need temporary lists, token values,
% sequences, Boolean flags, and integers. The implementation therefore enters
% LaTeX3 syntax and allocates a typed variable for every distinct role.
%
% |\ExplSyntaxOn| changes the programming syntax so underscores and colons may
% appear in control-sequence names and ordinary spaces are ignored. LaTeX3
% names describe both scope and data type. The prefix |g__kanban| denotes
% private global package state, whereas |l__kanban| denotes private local or
% temporary state. These prefixes state the intended scope; the local or
% global form of the assignment command still determines the actual TeX
% assignment. The suffixes |_prop|, |_clist|, |_tl|, |_seq|, |_bool|, and
% |_int| identify a property list, comma list, token list, sequence, Boolean,
% and integer respectively. Function names use a colon followed by an argument
% signature; for example, |:nN| means one braced token-list argument followed
% by one control-sequence argument.
%
% Each allocation command creates one variable and reports an error if that
% variable already exists. |\prop_new:N| creates a key--value mapping;
% |\clist_new:N| creates a comma-separated list; |\tl_new:N| creates a general
% token-list variable; |\seq_new:N| creates an ordered sequence;
% |\bool_new:N| creates a LaTeX3 Boolean; and |\int_new:N| creates an integer.
% The final |N| in each command signature means that the argument must be the
% variable control sequence itself, not its textual name.
% \Needspace{30\baselineskip}
%    \begin{macrocode}
\ExplSyntaxOn
\prop_new:N \g__kanban_palettes_prop
\prop_new:N \g__kanban_palette_texts_prop
\clist_new:N \g__kanban_palette_clist
\clist_new:N \g__kanban_palette_fg_clist
\tl_new:N \g__kanban_palette_name_tl
\clist_new:N \l__kanban_board_palette_clist
\clist_new:N \l__kanban_board_palette_fg_clist
\clist_new:N \l__kanban_palette_bg_clist
\clist_new:N \l__kanban_palette_fg_clist
\seq_new:N \l__kanban_palette_pair_seq
\bool_new:N \l__kanban_bad_palette_bool
\clist_new:N \g__kanban_columns_clist
\clist_new:N \l__kanban_board_columns_clist
\prop_new:N \l__kanban_column_ids_prop
\int_new:N \l__kanban_column_index_int
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_palette_name_tl
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_column_tl
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_column_id_tl
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_choice_tl
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_pending_startdate_tl
\tl_new:N \l__kanban_pending_reference_date_tl
\bool_new:N \l__kanban_pending_startdate_bool
\bool_new:N \l__kanban_pending_reference_date_bool
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The two global property lists form the palette registry: one stores heading
% backgrounds and the other stores optional heading foregrounds. Global comma
% lists hold the currently selected persistent palette and column defaults.
% Their local counterparts are the snapshot for an active board. The remaining
% local containers support palette-pair validation, column normalization,
% numeric column lookup, normalized choices, and the deferred processing of
% start and reference dates. Deferring those dates is necessary because a
% later |date-format| key in the same option list must be resolved before an
% ambiguous date is interpreted.
%
% \paragraph{The initial column model.}
% An environment with no |columns| key must still be valid. The package
% therefore installs the three canonical default stages as soon as the global
% column list has been allocated. The names are stored in normalized uppercase
% form so task assignment can be case-insensitive from the beginning.
%
% |\clist_gset:Nn| assigns a comma list globally. Its |N| argument is the
% destination variable, its |n| argument is literal braced input, and the |g|
% indicates that the assignment survives TeX grouping. Later setup commands
% use the same global list as the persistent source from which each board takes
% its local snapshot.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\clist_gset:Nn \g__kanban_columns_clist {BACKLOG,DOING,DONE}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% \paragraph{Named diagnostics.}
% The bootstrap stage finally defines reusable diagnostics for errors that may
% arise in several configuration scopes. Naming each diagnostic keeps its main
% message and corrective help text consistent whether invalid input originates
% in a package option, |\kbset|, or a board option.
%
% |\msg_new:nnnn| creates a LaTeX3 message. The first argument is the module
% name, the second is the message identifier, the third is the diagnostic text,
% and the fourth is explanatory help. Parameter markers such as |#1| and |#2|
% are placeholders supplied when the message is issued. Under
% |\ExplSyntaxOn|, a tilde represents an explicit interword space. Defining a
% message does not print it; later code selects its severity with commands such
% as |\msg_warning:nnnn| and supplies the placeholder values.
% \Needspace{14\baselineskip}
%    \begin{macrocode}
\msg_new:nnnn {kanban}{invalid-date-setting}
  {Invalid~#1~'#2';~keeping~the~current~value.}
  {Use~a~valid~Gregorian~date~in~one~of~the~documented~numeric~forms.}
\msg_new:nnnn {kanban}{invalid-dimension}
  {Invalid~#1~value~'#2';~keeping~the~current~value.}
  {Use~a~non-negative~explicit~dimension,~such~as~2mm,~or~a~registered
    ~LaTeX~length.}
\msg_new:nnnn {kanban}{unknown-key}
  {Unknown~#1~option~'#2';~the~option~was~ignored.}
  {Check~the~spelling~and~the~documented~scope~of~the~key.}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% At this point the package has allocated, but has not yet populated, the
% palette registries and board-local lookup structures. The following
% subsections define the functions that validate and populate those containers.
%
%
%    \begin{macrocode}
%</package>
%    \end{macrocode}
